1
00:00:17,010 --> 00:00:18,300
Whatcha doing?

2
00:00:18,310 --> 00:00:21,150
I'm attempting to view my work as a fleeting peripheral image

3
00:00:21,160 --> 00:00:24,060
so as to engage the superior colliculus of my brain.

4
00:00:27,780 --> 00:00:28,640
Interesting.

5
00:00:28,650 --> 00:00:30,440
I usually just have coffee.

6
00:00:31,590 --> 00:00:32,760
You've been up all night?

7
00:00:32,770 --> 00:00:33,850
Is it morning?

8
00:00:33,860 --> 00:00:34,570
Yes.

9
00:00:34,580 --> 00:00:36,250
Then I've been up all night.

10
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,360
And you're stuck?

11
00:00:40,370 --> 00:00:43,710
Why else would a person try to engage their superior colliculus?

12
00:00:44,400 --> 00:00:47,620
Oh, sorry, sweetie, I can't help you till I've had my coffee.

13
00:00:55,580 --> 00:00:57,770
Penny, I told you if you don't put him in his crate at night

14
00:00:57,780 --> 00:00:59,740
he just runs around the apartment.

15
00:01:03,560 --> 00:01:05,310
What is he doing now?

16
00:01:05,370 --> 00:01:08,330
Hmm, he's either isolating the terms

17
00:01:08,340 --> 00:01:11,600
of his formula and examining them individually,

18
00:01:11,630 --> 00:01:12,860
or...

19
00:01:13,090 --> 00:01:14,830
looking for the alligator that swallowed his hand

20
00:01:14,840 --> 00:01:16,740
after Peter Pan cut it off.

21
00:01:18,340 --> 00:01:20,610
Captain Hook's hand was eaten by a crocodile,

22
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:22,180
not an alligator.

23
00:01:23,070 --> 00:01:25,740
If you're going to mock me, at least get your facts straight.

24
00:01:26,630 --> 00:01:28,150
Aye, aye, Captain.

25
00:01:29,920 --> 00:01:31,620
I can't see it!

26
00:01:31,630 --> 00:01:33,280
It just won't coalesce.

27
00:01:33,290 --> 00:01:35,000
Maybe you need a fresh start.

28
00:01:35,850 --> 00:01:37,130
You're right.

29
00:01:49,050 --> 00:01:51,020
It's a great idea, Leonard. Thank you.

30
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:20,080
Electrons move through graphene...

31
00:02:20,090 --> 00:02:22,490
act as if they have no mass...

32
00:02:24,600 --> 00:02:26,430
How long has he been stuck?

33
00:02:26,440 --> 00:02:28,430
Intellectually about 30 hours.

34
00:02:28,440 --> 00:02:30,350
Emotionally about 29 years.

35
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:33,840
Unit cell contains two carbon atoms...

36
00:02:33,850 --> 00:02:38,600
Interior angle of a hexagon is 120 degrees.

37
00:02:40,510 --> 00:02:42,650
Have you tried rebooting him?

38
00:02:46,660 --> 00:02:48,910
No, I think it's a firmware problem.

39
00:02:50,260 --> 00:02:52,880
Hey, it's Disco Night at the Moonlight Roller Rink

40
00:02:52,890 --> 00:02:53,860
in Glendale tonight.

41
00:02:53,870 --> 00:02:55,470
Who's up for getting down?

42
00:02:56,430 --> 00:02:57,630
That's perfect.

43
00:02:57,640 --> 00:03:00,660
Bernadette's been hocking me to take her roller skating.

44
00:03:00,670 --> 00:03:02,060
I think Penny likes to skate.

45
00:03:02,070 --> 00:03:03,390
The four of us could double.

46
00:03:03,400 --> 00:03:04,700
What could be better? We're in.

47
00:03:04,710 --> 00:03:08,180
Great. It's not like I brought it up because I wanted to go.

48
00:03:11,360 --> 00:03:12,510
You can come with us.

49
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:13,960
No, it's okay. I don't have to go.

50
00:03:13,970 --> 00:03:15,480
I'm happy just to guide you and your ladies

51
00:03:15,490 --> 00:03:17,200
to suitable entertainment choices.

52
00:03:17,210 --> 00:03:19,880
I'm a walking brown Yelp.Com.

53
00:03:22,040 --> 00:03:25,010
Structure, constant structure. One atom...

54
00:03:25,230 --> 00:03:27,520
Boy, he's really gone, isn't he?

55
00:03:27,670 --> 00:03:31,030
Yeah, this morning he used a stick of butter as deodorant.

56
00:03:32,110 --> 00:03:34,950
I thought I smelled popcorn.

57
00:03:37,610 --> 00:03:39,750
Pattern is the same as fermions travels on the pathways...

58
00:03:39,760 --> 00:03:41,710
Hexagonal... It's always hexagonal...

59
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:43,820
I haven't seen him this stuck since he tried to figure out

60
00:03:43,830 --> 00:03:45,580
the third Matrix movie.

61
00:03:48,560 --> 00:03:51,010
Hey, those are my lima beans!

62
00:03:51,020 --> 00:03:53,320
Not lima beans, carbon atoms.

63
00:03:53,850 --> 00:03:55,350
But if I don't eat my lima beans,

64
00:03:55,360 --> 00:03:57,600
I can't have my cookie.

65
00:03:58,150 --> 00:03:59,300
Here, you want my peas?

66
00:03:59,310 --> 00:04:00,260
The peas-- perfect.

67
00:04:00,270 --> 00:04:01,930
They can be electrons.

68
00:04:02,260 --> 00:04:03,570
Want my corn?

69
00:04:03,580 --> 00:04:05,480
Don't be ridiculous.

70
00:04:06,720 --> 00:04:08,200
What would I do with corn?

71
00:04:09,630 --> 00:04:12,270
So roller skating-- should we all grab a bite to eat first?

72
00:04:12,280 --> 00:04:13,420
Good. P.F. Chang's?

73
00:04:13,430 --> 00:04:14,840
My mom has coupons.

74
00:04:14,850 --> 00:04:16,480
Great.

75
00:04:17,060 --> 00:04:18,750
Your mom's not coming, right?

76
00:04:18,760 --> 00:04:20,530
Not this time, I promise.

77
00:04:21,640 --> 00:04:24,700
Okay, just to be clear, roller skating was my idea,

78
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:27,010
and I'm very unhappy that you turned it into a double date,

79
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:28,540
and I hope you both fall on your asses

80
00:04:28,550 --> 00:04:30,310
and break your coccyxes.

81
00:04:32,850 --> 00:04:35,530
The plural of coccyx is coccyges.

82
00:04:36,570 --> 00:04:38,780
Screw you. Give me back my lima beans.

83
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:44,190
Oh, my God. Have you ever been so embarrassed?

84
00:04:44,200 --> 00:04:45,470
Not recently.

85
00:04:45,480 --> 00:04:46,820
I don't know which was lamer:

86
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:49,310
their roller-skating or their disco dancing.

87
00:04:49,310 --> 00:04:50,460
For me, the worst part

88
00:04:50,470 --> 00:04:53,040
was when people saw us leave with them.

89
00:04:54,700 --> 00:04:57,970
You had some nice moves out there, Howard.

90
00:04:58,410 --> 00:04:59,690
Thanks. You, too.

91
00:04:59,700 --> 00:05:02,100
Did you notice all the people looking at us?

92
00:05:03,080 --> 00:05:05,770
Not really. I was in my boogie zone.

93
00:05:07,820 --> 00:05:09,920
When Howard tried to do the splits...

94
00:05:09,930 --> 00:05:11,310
Shh.

95
00:05:11,330 --> 00:05:13,160
Sorry. I'm moving a little slow.

96
00:05:13,170 --> 00:05:14,960
I think I bruised my coccyx.

97
00:05:14,970 --> 00:05:16,640
Oh, poor baby.

98
00:05:16,660 --> 00:05:18,400
Don't tell Koothrappali.

99
00:05:19,150 --> 00:05:20,120
After you.

100
00:05:20,130 --> 00:05:21,590
Oh, what a gentleman.

101
00:05:21,600 --> 00:05:23,050
Hey, Sheldon.

102
00:05:24,070 --> 00:05:24,780
Oh, my God!

103
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:26,030
Are you...

104
00:05:26,860 --> 00:05:27,930
Good Lord!

105
00:05:27,940 --> 00:05:29,690
You're ruining everything!

106
00:05:29,700 --> 00:05:30,620
Oh, damn.

107
00:05:30,630 --> 00:05:31,530
Are you okay?

108
00:05:31,540 --> 00:05:32,890
Do I look okay?

109
00:05:32,900 --> 00:05:34,800
Don't bark at me. I fell, too.

110
00:05:34,810 --> 00:05:37,270
Oh, you've been falling all night. You're used to it.

111
00:05:38,990 --> 00:05:40,750
Sheldon, what the hell are you doing?

112
00:05:40,760 --> 00:05:42,510
The same thing I've been doing for three days.

113
00:05:42,520 --> 00:05:44,090
Trying to figure out why electrons

114
00:05:44,100 --> 00:05:45,690
behave as if they have no mass

115
00:05:45,700 --> 00:05:47,670
when traveling through a graphene sheet.

116
00:05:47,680 --> 00:05:48,920
With marbles?

117
00:05:48,930 --> 00:05:49,750
Well, I needed something bigger

118
00:05:49,760 --> 00:05:51,590
than peas, now, didn't I?

119
00:05:52,770 --> 00:05:55,670
Sheldon, when was the last time you got any sleep?

120
00:05:55,680 --> 00:05:57,110
I don't know-- two, three days.

121
00:05:57,120 --> 00:05:57,690
Not important.

122
00:05:57,700 --> 00:06:00,430
I don't need sleep. I need answers.

123
00:06:00,440 --> 00:06:02,040
I need to determine where

124
00:06:02,050 --> 00:06:04,870
in this swamp of unbalanced formulas

125
00:06:04,880 --> 00:06:07,160
squatteth the toad of truth.

126
00:06:09,340 --> 00:06:10,620
Toad of truth?

127
00:06:10,630 --> 00:06:12,090
Is that a physics thing?

128
00:06:12,100 --> 00:06:14,220
No, that's a crazy thing.

129
00:06:15,300 --> 00:06:16,720
Okay, Sheldon.

130
00:06:16,730 --> 00:06:19,290
What happens to our neuroreceptors

131
00:06:19,300 --> 00:06:22,260
when we don't get enough REM sleep?

132
00:06:23,280 --> 00:06:26,570
They lose their sensitivity to serotonin and norepinephrine.

133
00:06:26,580 --> 00:06:28,190
Which leads to...?

134
00:06:28,320 --> 00:06:30,770
Impaired cognitive function.

135
00:06:30,850 --> 00:06:35,560
Right, so march in there, brush your teeth and go to bed.

136
00:06:35,970 --> 00:06:37,730
But I don't want to go to bed.

137
00:06:40,060 --> 00:06:42,300
I'm going to count to three. One...

138
00:06:42,310 --> 00:06:43,990
Oh, all right.

139
00:06:47,960 --> 00:06:50,490
That was amazing how you handled him.

140
00:06:50,500 --> 00:06:52,650
I know how to deal with stubborn children.

141
00:06:52,660 --> 00:06:56,190
My mother used to run an illegal day care center in our basement.

142
00:07:09,320 --> 00:07:12,800
Leonard, you're... you're giggling in your sleep.

143
00:07:12,810 --> 00:07:13,810
It's not me.

144
00:07:13,820 --> 00:07:15,410
It's my new ringtone.

145
00:07:15,420 --> 00:07:18,270
The Joker from Batman.

146
00:07:18,850 --> 00:07:20,530
Well, it creeps me out.

147
00:07:20,540 --> 00:07:23,150
Me, too, but I paid three bucks for it.

148
00:07:23,940 --> 00:07:25,530
Just answer the phone.

149
00:07:30,320 --> 00:07:31,600
Hello.

150
00:07:33,130 --> 00:07:34,950
Yeah, I'm Leonard Hofstadter.

151
00:07:36,850 --> 00:07:38,890
Yeah, yeah, he's my roommate.

152
00:07:39,980 --> 00:07:41,850
Oh, God, is he okay?

153
00:07:43,750 --> 00:07:45,930
Yeah, all-all right. I'll be right there.

154
00:07:46,280 --> 00:07:47,420
What happened?

155
00:07:47,430 --> 00:07:50,380
Sheldon's escaped and is terrorizing the village.

156
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,100
Okay. Have fun.

157
00:07:58,130 --> 00:07:59,640
Hi. I'm Dr. Hofstadter.

158
00:07:59,650 --> 00:08:02,020
- Where is he? - Ball pit.

159
00:08:05,570 --> 00:08:07,300
Thanks for not calling the cops.

160
00:08:07,310 --> 00:08:08,590
Oh, hey, it's no big deal.

161
00:08:08,600 --> 00:08:10,980
My sister's got a kid who's special.

162
00:08:12,030 --> 00:08:14,630
ah, well, he's extra special.

163
00:08:17,000 --> 00:08:18,430
Hey, Shelly.

164
00:08:19,310 --> 00:08:20,430
What you doing?

165
00:08:20,440 --> 00:08:22,050
Size ratio was all wrong.

166
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:23,210
Couldn't visualize it.

167
00:08:23,220 --> 00:08:24,970
Needed bigger carbon atoms.

168
00:08:26,180 --> 00:08:27,620
Sure, sure.

169
00:08:29,540 --> 00:08:31,790
How did you get into this place?

170
00:08:31,820 --> 00:08:34,340
Back door has a five-pin tumbler system,

171
00:08:34,350 --> 00:08:36,840
single-circuit alarm. Child's play.

172
00:08:37,540 --> 00:08:40,670
You can start sorting protons and neutrons

173
00:08:40,680 --> 00:08:42,600
while I build carbon atoms.

174
00:08:42,910 --> 00:08:44,100
No, I don't think so.

175
00:08:44,110 --> 00:08:46,070
We need to go home now.

176
00:08:46,530 --> 00:08:48,390
But I'm still working.

177
00:08:48,470 --> 00:08:49,750
If you don't come out of there,

178
00:08:49,760 --> 00:08:52,040
I'm going to have to drag you out.

179
00:08:52,390 --> 00:08:55,870
You can try, but you'll never catch me.

180
00:08:59,360 --> 00:09:01,640
For God's sakes.

181
00:09:06,640 --> 00:09:08,850
Sheldon, come here!

182
00:09:08,870 --> 00:09:10,170
Bazinga.

183
00:09:12,560 --> 00:09:13,930
Bazinga.

184
00:09:15,800 --> 00:09:17,150
Bazinga.

185
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:20,560
Bazinga.

186
00:09:21,580 --> 00:09:23,050
Bazinga.

187
00:09:30,500 --> 00:09:31,690
Leonard and Penny.

188
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:32,780
Leonard and Penny.

189
00:09:32,790 --> 00:09:34,920
What? What, what, what?

190
00:09:39,700 --> 00:09:41,170
Leonard and Penny.

191
00:09:42,560 --> 00:09:43,780
What?

192
00:09:43,790 --> 00:09:45,140
I have good news.

193
00:09:45,150 --> 00:09:46,750
And you had to barge in here and wake us up

194
00:09:46,760 --> 00:09:48,270
in the middle of the night?

195
00:09:48,280 --> 00:09:50,080
Your cell phone was off.

196
00:09:50,270 --> 00:09:52,760
Because we didn't want to be disturbed.

197
00:09:53,010 --> 00:09:55,200
Well, that didn't work out, did it?

198
00:09:55,910 --> 00:09:58,040
Sheldon, what do you want?

199
00:09:58,050 --> 00:10:01,360
I came to tell you I've got the answer.

200
00:10:02,080 --> 00:10:03,370
Really?

201
00:10:03,380 --> 00:10:05,480
You figured out the graphene problem?

202
00:10:05,490 --> 00:10:07,370
No, no, I'm still hopelessly stuck on that,

203
00:10:07,380 --> 00:10:10,940
but I figured out how to figure it out.

204
00:10:10,950 --> 00:10:12,390
Okay, you know what, Leonard, I know I said

205
00:10:12,400 --> 00:10:14,010
I could handle your roommate, but I was wrong.

206
00:10:14,020 --> 00:10:15,620
We're going to have to break up.

207
00:10:16,450 --> 00:10:18,350
What are you talking about?

208
00:10:22,530 --> 00:10:24,070
Einstein.

209
00:10:24,490 --> 00:10:26,340
Yeah, I'm going to need a little more.

210
00:10:27,300 --> 00:10:29,180
Albert Einstein.

211
00:10:31,940 --> 00:10:33,410
Keep going.

212
00:10:33,420 --> 00:10:36,240
When Albert Einstein came up with special relativity,

213
00:10:36,250 --> 00:10:38,720
he was working at the patent office.

214
00:10:38,730 --> 00:10:41,080
So, you're going to go work at the patent office?

215
00:10:41,090 --> 00:10:42,600
Don't be absurd.

216
00:10:42,610 --> 00:10:44,010
That's in Washington.

217
00:10:44,020 --> 00:10:46,210
You know I could never live in a city whose streets

218
00:10:46,220 --> 00:10:48,100
are laid out in a wheel-and-spoke pattern.

219
00:10:48,110 --> 00:10:50,080
No. I'm going to find

220
00:10:50,090 --> 00:10:51,820
a similarly menial job

221
00:10:51,830 --> 00:10:54,960
where my basal ganglia are occupied with a routine task,

222
00:10:54,970 --> 00:10:56,590
freeing my prefrontal cortex

223
00:10:56,600 --> 00:10:59,390
to work quietly in the background on my problem.

224
00:11:00,280 --> 00:11:01,460
Sounds like a great plan.

225
00:11:01,470 --> 00:11:02,490
Of course it is.

226
00:11:02,500 --> 00:11:03,560
Even talking to you is

227
00:11:03,570 --> 00:11:04,920
sufficiently menial that I can feel

228
00:11:04,930 --> 00:11:07,290
the proverbial juices starting to flow.

229
00:11:08,020 --> 00:11:10,380
Okay, well, thanks for sharing with us.

230
00:11:10,570 --> 00:11:12,450
- Good night.  - You're welcome.

231
00:11:12,460 --> 00:11:14,210
And good night to you, too.

232
00:11:14,950 --> 00:11:17,840
Oh, by the way, I was watching you sleep for a moment,

233
00:11:17,850 --> 00:11:19,380
and I noticed that your snoring

234
00:11:20,150 --> 00:11:21,580
seems to be worse when you're on your back.

235
00:11:21,590 --> 00:11:23,480
Leonard doesn't snore.

236
00:11:23,490 --> 00:11:25,490
No, I wasn't talking to Leonard.

237
00:11:28,300 --> 00:11:29,730
Told you.

238
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:35,370
So, Mr. Cooper, you're looking for a job.

239
00:11:35,380 --> 00:11:37,180
A menial job.

240
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:39,790
Like yours.

241
00:11:41,570 --> 00:11:43,750
Well, thank you for noticing.

242
00:11:44,910 --> 00:11:47,430
I'm "Menial Employee of the Month."

243
00:11:49,370 --> 00:11:51,870
Do you have a particular field in mind?

244
00:11:51,880 --> 00:11:52,890
I do.

245
00:11:52,900 --> 00:11:54,410
For thousands of years,

246
00:11:54,420 --> 00:11:56,500
the lowest classes of the human race

247
00:11:56,510 --> 00:11:59,380
have spent their lives laboring to erect monuments

248
00:11:59,390 --> 00:12:00,900
under the lash of their betters,

249
00:12:00,910 --> 00:12:02,850
until finally, they dropped down

250
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:06,680
and became one with the dust through which they trudged.

251
00:12:07,130 --> 00:12:09,070
Do you have anything like that?

252
00:12:11,090 --> 00:12:12,650
No.

253
00:12:13,370 --> 00:12:15,580
Shouldn't you check your database?

254
00:12:17,310 --> 00:12:18,690
No.

255
00:12:22,330 --> 00:12:24,210
You didn't really type?

256
00:12:25,100 --> 00:12:27,170
I didn't really have to.

257
00:12:28,110 --> 00:12:29,900
So, how about construction?

258
00:12:29,910 --> 00:12:31,960
Oh, that would be good!

259
00:12:31,970 --> 00:12:35,590
Sawing, hammering, eating out of a lunch pail

260
00:12:35,600 --> 00:12:37,210
as my working-class fellows and I

261
00:12:37,220 --> 00:12:41,690
sit perched precariously on a girder high above the metropolis.

262
00:12:42,760 --> 00:12:45,090
No, no. This is putting up sheetrock

263
00:12:45,100 --> 00:12:47,640
at a housing project in Rosemead.

264
00:12:48,660 --> 00:12:49,620
I could do that.

265
00:12:49,630 --> 00:12:51,020
- Good.  - One question.

266
00:12:51,030 --> 00:12:53,140
- Yes?  - What's sheetrock?

267
00:12:54,230 --> 00:12:55,950
Moving on.

268
00:12:56,940 --> 00:12:59,810
How about doing deliveries for a florist?

269
00:12:59,820 --> 00:13:01,220
That seems acceptable.

270
00:13:01,230 --> 00:13:03,640
- Do you have your own car?  - I don't drive.

271
00:13:04,760 --> 00:13:06,370
Of course you don't.

272
00:13:07,430 --> 00:13:10,730
Mr. Cooper, let me just ask you a question.

273
00:13:10,740 --> 00:13:12,270
What was your last job?

274
00:13:12,280 --> 00:13:14,810
Senior theoretical particle physicist at CalTech,

275
00:13:14,820 --> 00:13:18,750
focusing on M theory, or, in layman's terms, string theory.

276
00:13:19,440 --> 00:13:20,830
I see.

277
00:13:21,350 --> 00:13:23,180
Just give me a second.

278
00:13:26,430 --> 00:13:28,950
Security!

279
00:13:34,190 --> 00:13:37,020
Okay, I'll get those drinks started for you.

280
00:13:37,860 --> 00:13:39,410
Behind you.

281
00:13:40,590 --> 00:13:43,450
Sheldon, what the hell are you doing?

282
00:13:43,460 --> 00:13:46,520
I'm trying to get these tables cleared. We're slammed.

283
00:13:47,340 --> 00:13:48,680
No, wait, wait, no, wait.

284
00:13:48,690 --> 00:13:50,550
Wh-What are you doing here?

285
00:13:50,640 --> 00:13:52,200
A reasonable question.

286
00:13:52,210 --> 00:13:53,640
I asked myself,

287
00:13:53,650 --> 00:13:57,040
what is the most mind-numbing, pedeststrian job conceivable,

288
00:13:57,050 --> 00:13:58,440
and three answers came to mind:

289
00:13:58,450 --> 00:14:01,760
uh, toll booth attendant, Apple Store genius...

290
00:14:03,610 --> 00:14:05,050
...and what Penny does.

291
00:14:05,060 --> 00:14:06,370
Now...

292
00:14:07,240 --> 00:14:09,630
since I don't like  touching other people's coins,

293
00:14:09,640 --> 00:14:13,980
and I refuse to contribute to the devaluation of the word "Genius,"

294
00:14:14,570 --> 00:14:16,120
here I am.

295
00:14:16,860 --> 00:14:19,860
You just, you just walked in and they hired you, just like that?

296
00:14:19,870 --> 00:14:21,200
Oh, heavens, no.

297
00:14:21,210 --> 00:14:24,360
Since I don't need to be paid, I didn't need to be hired.

298
00:14:24,370 --> 00:14:27,820
I simply came in, picked up a tray, and started "Working for the man."

299
00:14:29,240 --> 00:14:31,110
Let me get that plate out of your way.

300
00:14:31,250 --> 00:14:34,200
- Sheldon,this is ridiculous. - Is it?

301
00:14:34,210 --> 00:14:36,070
Just a moment ago, I had a minor epiphany

302
00:14:36,080 --> 00:14:38,220
regarding the polymer degradation phenomenon

303
00:14:38,230 --> 00:14:41,170
while scraping congealed nachos off a plate.

304
00:14:42,040 --> 00:14:43,820
Bernadette, table 10 wants their check.

305
00:14:43,830 --> 00:14:44,720
Thanks, Sheldon.

306
00:14:44,730 --> 00:14:46,730
Sheldon,wait, this isn't even what I do.

307
00:14:46,740 --> 00:14:48,850
I'm a waitress, not a busboy.

308
00:14:49,120 --> 00:14:51,380
You're right. That is more menial.

309
00:14:52,650 --> 00:14:55,100
Hello, I'm Sheldon. I'll be your server today.

310
00:14:56,170 --> 00:14:58,060
I don't recommend the salmon.

311
00:14:58,070 --> 00:14:59,840
I saw it in the kitchen.

312
00:15:02,340 --> 00:15:06,270
All right, one bacon cheeseburger,

313
00:15:06,280 --> 00:15:10,100
breaking two Jewish dietary restrictions simultaneously--

314
00:15:10,230 --> 00:15:11,790
kudos.

315
00:15:12,210 --> 00:15:14,300
Beer-battered fish and chips.

316
00:15:14,310 --> 00:15:16,330
Now, here's your tartar sauce.

317
00:15:16,340 --> 00:15:17,610
I also brought you salsa.

318
00:15:17,620 --> 00:15:19,590
It's a little unconventional, but I think you'll like it.

319
00:15:19,600 --> 00:15:21,060
It's zingy.

320
00:15:22,540 --> 00:15:25,220
And for you, Factory Burrito Grande--

321
00:15:25,230 --> 00:15:26,890
no cheese, no sour cream,

322
00:15:26,900 --> 00:15:30,210
no ugly consequences from your lactose intolerance.

323
00:15:31,090 --> 00:15:32,570
Bon appetit.

324
00:15:33,030 --> 00:15:34,330
Hang on.

325
00:15:35,180 --> 00:15:37,040
- Black beans, not pinto beans? - Yes.

326
00:15:37,050 --> 00:15:38,270
- Double guacamole? - Of course.

327
00:15:38,280 --> 00:15:39,600
- No cilantro? - Nope.

328
00:15:39,610 --> 00:15:41,100
- Lettuce shredded, not chopped? - Yep.

329
00:15:41,110 --> 00:15:42,500
You understand why I'm doing this to you?

330
00:15:42,510 --> 00:15:44,950
- I do. - That'll be all.

331
00:15:47,630 --> 00:15:49,130
That was fun.

332
00:15:51,200 --> 00:15:52,800
How long can he keep this up?

333
00:15:52,810 --> 00:15:54,600
I heard about this professor at MIT

334
00:15:54,610 --> 00:15:55,880
who melted down, bought a van,

335
00:15:55,890 --> 00:15:58,720
and spent the rest of his life as a mobile dog groomer.

336
00:15:58,730 --> 00:16:00,370
He never went back to the university?

337
00:16:00,380 --> 00:16:03,680
Only to shampoo Professor Shamburg's shitzu

338
00:16:04,580 --> 00:16:05,920
Sheesh.

339
00:16:08,400 --> 00:16:12,090
I bet if we all chipped in, we could buy Sheldon a van.

340
00:16:12,260 --> 00:16:13,530
But he's afraid of dogs.

341
00:16:13,540 --> 00:16:16,290
Yeah, that's the only thing wrong with that plan.

342
00:16:17,330 --> 00:16:20,490
Hey, guys, sorry you had to wait, but we are swamped.

343
00:16:21,380 --> 00:16:22,700
What's this?

344
00:16:22,710 --> 00:16:24,430
Sheldon took our order.

345
00:16:24,440 --> 00:16:26,720
Sheldon doesn't work here.

346
00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:29,290
Well, honey, not to complain,

347
00:16:29,300 --> 00:16:31,590
but we were starting to think you didn't either.

348
00:16:35,060 --> 00:16:36,960
Is that really necessary?

349
00:16:38,810 --> 00:16:40,780
Good Lord.

350
00:16:41,530 --> 00:16:44,170
The interference pattern in the fracture.

351
00:16:44,640 --> 00:16:47,260
The motion of the wave through the molecular structure.

352
00:16:47,270 --> 00:16:49,260
I've been looking at it all wrong.

353
00:16:49,270 --> 00:16:51,760
I can't consider the electrons as particles.

354
00:16:51,770 --> 00:16:55,090
They move through the graphene as a wave.

355
00:16:55,730 --> 00:16:57,910
It's a wave!

356
00:16:59,940 --> 00:17:02,430
The moment to applaud would be now.

357
00:17:04,740 --> 00:17:06,370
Troglodytes.

358
00:17:07,630 --> 00:17:09,340
Sheldon... where are you going?

359
00:17:09,350 --> 00:17:10,740
Aren't you going to clean this up?

360
00:17:10,750 --> 00:17:12,540
I'm sorry. I don't work here.

361
00:17:26,010 --> 00:17:28,610
- Happy now? - I'm on a cloud.

362
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:30,430
Swing me.

363
00:17:54,200 --> 00:17:56,050
Sheldon, come here.

364
00:17:57,400 --> 00:17:58,780
Bazinga.

365
00:18:06,360 --> 00:18:07,910
Bazinga.

366
00:18:11,100 --> 00:18:12,510
Bazinga.

367
00:18:14,610 --> 00:18:16,040
Bazinga.

368
00:18:19,240 --> 00:18:21,270
Bazinga!

